The Week 6 lab introduces classification with PySpark using the Iris dataset, one of the most well-known datasets in machine learning. The Iris dataset contains measurements of sepal length, sepal width, petal length, and petal width for three species of iris flowers. The aim is to classify flower samples into their correct species using machine learning models.

The code begins by setting up a Spark session in Google Colab, which allows distributed processing of data.To prepare the dataset for modeling, all four measurement columns are combined into a single feature vector using the VectorAssembler transformation. Since the class column is stored as text labels, StringIndexer is applied to convert species names into numerical values, making them usable for machine learning algorithms. The dataset is then split into two parts: 70% for training and 30% for testing, ensuring that models are evaluated on unseen data.

Two machine learning models are trained and compared. The first is a Decision Tree classifier, configured to use entropy as its impurity measure and limited to a maximum depth of 4. The second is a Logistic Regression model applied in a One-vs-Rest (OvR) setting, which adapts logistic regression for multi-class classification. Both models are trained on the training data and tested on the testing data.

The evaluation is done using accuracy, which measures how many predictions match the actual labels. The Decision Tree’s accuracy and the Logistic Regression OvR accuracy are both printed, allowing for direct comparison between the two approaches. Finally, predictions from the logistic regression model are extracted and converted to a Pandas DataFrame to build a confusion matrix. This matrix, displayed as a heatmap, provides detailed insight into which species were predicted correctly and where misclassifications occurred.

In summary, this lab demonstrates the complete workflow of classification in PySpark: loading and preparing data, transforming features and labels, training multiple models, evaluating performance, and visualizing results. It highlights the power of PySpark for handling structured data and compares two different algorithms to show the trade-offs in classification accuracy.
